


“Do you need me at the gate now?”




“Start toward us.”




“I’m leaving.”




Maren ended the call and put the phone away.




Jonah had moved several yards down the trail. He stopped when he realized she was not beside him.




“Stay where I can reach you,” she said.




He waited.




They walked without filming. The white triangles led them back through the river corridor, then toward the junction where the two solo routes divided. The farther they moved from the rise, the easier it became to hear the river below them. Frogs called in short bursts below the bank. Crickets filled the drier slope. Once, three heavy splashes came from the water or the opposite mudflat, then stopped.




Jonah looked toward the river.




Maren kept walking.




At the northern junction, the route sheet still lay beside the blue-route tripod where she had dropped it. The camera on the tripod had continued recording the empty marker, its battery light blinking red.




She stopped long enough to turn it off.




Jonah stared at the lens. “How long did you leave that?”




“Since I ran after you.”




“You should keep that card separate.”




“I’m keeping every card separate.”




He nodded once and reached toward the tripod latch, then stopped when Maren folded it herself.




Maren folded the tripod and clipped the route sheet to her bag rather than smoothing it out. Her grease-pencil notes ended in the middle of the solo control. The last complete line was Jonah: white four.




The shelter appeared through the trees, the work light dark. The generator had been off since before the split. The emitter remained packed inside the equipment case where Maren had watched Jonah put it hours earlier. Without either device operating, the clearing held the same uneven insect noise as the trail. A frog called somewhere beyond the downhill edge. Another answered farther away.




Maren set the tripod beside the table and called Ruth Halvorsen.




Ruth’s voice was rough with sleep. “Lena called. Tell me where you are.”




Maren gave her the route, marker number, approximate direction off trail, the distance up the rise, and the phone’s last visible position. She told her they had crossed the boundary after Jonah stopped responding. She did not describe the stone as a grave, marker, foundation, or anything else she could not establish.




“You’re both out of that area now?” Ruth asked.




“Yes.”




“You stay out. I’ll recover the phone after sunrise. I don’t want either of you trying to retrace an off-trail line in the dark.”




“I agree.”




“Can you send me the map pin you had when you were up there?”




“The accuracy circle was wide.”




“Send it anyway, and a still showing the first place he left the trail.”




“I have that.”




Ruth was quiet for a second. “Do I need medical help waiting at the gate?”




Maren looked at Jonah.




He had sat on the edge of the shelter table. His shoulders were down now, and he was unwrapping the loose end of the bandage with his teeth.




“The hand needs cleaning again,” Maren said. “He’s walking. He didn’t lose consciousness.”




“Your call on urgent care. I’ll meet you here at eight.”




Maren ended the call.




Jonah held out his hand without being asked. The reopened cut was shallow but wet. Maren used the remaining saline, gauze, and tape from the first-aid pouch. He watched her work instead of watching the tree line.




When she finished, he flexed his fingers once.




“The phone is still recording,” he said.




“It was when we left it.”




“That’s clean coverage.”




“We’ll know what it caught when Ruth brings it back.”




Jonah looked toward the dark trail.




Then he looked down at the new bandage.




He said nothing.




Maren closed the first-aid pouch. “We are not reviewing anything here.”




He did not argue.




They packed the shelter in eleven minutes. Maren checked every card against the equipment log and placed them in separate labeled sleeves: fixed shelter, trail recorder, blue control, main camera, Jonah handheld. She wrote MISSING — JONAH PHONE beside the final line and added RECOVERY BY R. HALVORSEN / DAYLIGHT beneath it.




Jonah carried nothing heavier than his handheld recorder.




At the clearing edge, he stopped.




He looked toward the trail, then raised the recorder to his mouth and pressed its button.




“Closing note,” he said.




She stood several feet away with the equipment bag over one shoulder.




Jonah looked toward the trail rather than at her. “We came back because what happened after the scouting visit did not stop when we left. Tonight the place warned us early, then pushed us away from the shelter. When we separated, the activity followed me to the stone. It approached when I turned my light off. Maren heard it from multiple directions. When we left, it stopped following.”




Maren tightened her grip on the bag strap.




Jonah continued. “The animal calls are back now. The pressure is gone. Whatever happened up there wanted us out, and we are leaving.”




He lowered the recorder but did not stop it.




“Tell me you heard the movement on both sides,” he said.




Maren held out her hand. “Give me the recorder.”




“You heard it.”




“I heard enough to know I’m not doing your closing note.”




Jonah looked at the recorder.




He did not hand it over.




Maren kept her hand out.




“Then stop recording,” she said.




His thumb rested over the stop button.




He held her gaze for another second.




Jonah pressed stop.




For several seconds he held the recorder without putting it away.




“You could have just said no,” he said.




“I did.”




He slid the recorder into his jacket pocket.




They left the shelter.




The route to the entrance took less time than the walk in. They did not stop for prompts or baselines. Maren kept the light low on the trail and checked Jonah at each turn. He stayed beside her. The frogs continued below the riverbank, then faded as the trail climbed toward the prairie. Crickets replaced them. Near the entrance, moths struck the camera bag and Lena’s headlights showed through the trees beyond the locked vehicle gate.




Lena was out of the car before they reached it.




She looked first at Jonah’s bandaged hand, then at Maren. “What happened?”




“We broke the route rules,” Maren said. “He left the trail. I followed. We left his phone behind. Ruth is getting it in the morning.”




Jonah opened his mouth.




Maren handed Lena the equipment bag. “Can you put this in the trunk?”




Lena took it without asking another question.




Jonah waited until the bag was out of Maren’s hands. “There was a stone up there. Something was moving around it.”




Lena looked at Maren.




“There was a rectangular stone or piece of masonry,” Maren said. “We heard movement in the brush, but we never saw what caused it.”




Jonah gave a short laugh. “There. That’s the whole night.”




Maren did not answer.




The gate stayed locked behind them. Ruth had told them to use the pedestrian exit and leave the equipment pickup for morning if necessary, but Lena had parked outside the boundary, and the remaining gear fit in the car. Maren logged their exit time at 12:31 and photographed the locked gate with her phone.
